   347|                <h1>Dental Implant Cost in China 2026: Complete Price Guide for International Patients</h1>
   348|                <div class="post-meta">
   349|                    <span>May 4, 2026</strong> | <span>Dental Care</strong> | <span>By China Hospitals Guide Team</strong>
   350|                </div>
   351|            </header>
   352|
   353|            <p class="lead">
   354|                A retired teacher from Vancouver was quoted $4,200 per implant at her local clinic. She flew to Beijing instead and got the same Straumann implant, placed by a board-certified prosthodontist, for $1,350 each. Total bill including flights, hotel, and two weeks of recovery? Less than half what she'd have paid at home for a single implant. That's the deal China is offering in 2026 — and the market just got even more competitive.
   355|            </p>
   356|
   357|            <h2>Why 2026 Is a Different Year for Dental Implants in China</h2>
   358|
   359|            <p>On April 29, 2026, Straumann — the Swiss premium implant maker — reported that it beat Q1 forecasts globally while confirming it faces ongoing \"procurement uncertainty\" in China. The company's shares rose on the news. What does that mean for patients? China's government volume-based procurement program is squeezing domestic prices, and private hospitals are now competing aggressively for international patients — driving quality up and costs down. For anyone considering dental implants, the timing has never been better.</p>
   360|
   361|            <p>Peking University School of Stomatology opened a dedicated dental centre in Hainan Province in January 2026. Hainan is China's only duty-free island and already offers visa-free entry for citizens of dozens of countries. A dental implant procedure at a PKU-affiliated facility, combined with a beachside recovery — it's not a bad recovery plan.</p>
   362|
   363|            <p>Here's something the dental industry rarely mentions: China has been running a national volume-based procurement program since 2023 that has slashed implant prices by roughly 50%. The government negotiates directly with manufacturers on behalf of public hospitals, and those savings pass through to patients.</p>
   364|
   365|            <p>Meanwhile, Korean dental device manufacturers are expanding into other Asian markets because China-based demand has softened — a signal that Chinese domestic supply is saturating and foreign patients are the growth target. The Grand View Research China Dental Tourism Market report (October 2024) projects steady growth through 2033, with cost competitiveness being the primary draw.</p>
   366|
   367|            <h2>Dental Implant Costs in China vs. the World (2026)</h2>
   368|
