   425|            <div class="highlight">
   426|                <strong>What Chinese hospital implant packages typically include:</strong>
   427|                <ul style="margin: 15px 0 0 20px;">
   428|                    <li>3D CT scan and comprehensive consultation</li>
   429|                    <li>Implant post, abutment, and temporary crown</li>
   430|                    <li>Surgical procedure under local anesthesia or sedation</li>
   431|                    <li>Post-operative medications</li>
   432|                    <li>Follow-up appointments during your stay</li>
   433|                    <li>English-speaking patient coordinator</li>
   434|                </ul>
   435|                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><em>Note: Permanent crown placement typically requires a separate procedure 3-6 months after initial implant placement, once osseointegration is complete. Some hospitals bundle this; others quote it separately ($300-$600 per crown).</em></p>
   436|            </div>
   437|
   438|            <h2>What's Actually Happening in China's Dental Market (2026)</h2>
   439|
   440|            <p>China's volume-based procurement (VBP) program, which began rolling out in 2023, was designed to make dental implants more affordable for domestic patients. The program works by the government acting as a single buyer and negotiating bulk pricing. By 2025, the program had driven down prices at public hospitals significantly.</p>
   441|
   442|            <p>For international patients, the effect is indirect but real. Private hospitals catering to foreign patients now compete aggressively on price to attract medical tourists, since the VBP program has made domestic patients more price-sensitive. Straumann noted in their February 2026 earnings that China remains a "procurement uncertainty" — which is a polite way of saying the pricing landscape is shifting and they're adapting.</p>
   443|
   444|            <p>Separately, 3D printed medical devices are becoming more common in Chinese dental clinics, which further reduces per-unit costs for custom abutments and surgical guides. The IndexBox market report (May 2026) shows China's 3D printed medical devices market growing, with dental applications being a significant segment.</p>
   445|
   446|            <h2>Top Stomatology Hospitals for Dental Implants in China</h2>
   447|
   448|            <div class="hospital-card">
   449|                <h3>1. Peking University School of Stomatology — Beijing</h3>
   450|                <span class="tag">#1 Dental School in China</strong>
   451|                <span class="tag">JCI Accredited</strong>
   452|                <span class="tag">Since 1941</strong>
   453|                
   454|                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Why patients choose it:</strong> Peking University School of Stomatology is the Harvard of Chinese dentistry. They train specialists from across Asia and publish extensively in international journals. Their implantology department handles complex cases — severe bone loss, immediate loading, zygomatic implants — that general clinics won't touch.</p>
   455|                
   456|                <p><strong>Implants available:</strong> Straumann, Nobel Biocare, Astra Tech, Ankylos, plus Chinese premium brands (CAMLOG, BEGO)</p>
   457|                <p><strong>Cost range:</strong> $1,200 - $2,500 per standard implant; $2,000 - $4,000 per premium implant</p>
   458|                <p><strong>International services:</strong> Dedicated foreign patient center with English coordinators, digital records accessible globally, partnerships with international insurers</p>
   459|            </div>
   460|
   461|            <div class="hospital-card">
   462|                <h3>2. Shanghai Ninth People's Hospital (Ninth Hospital) — Shanghai</h3>
   463|                <span class="tag">Top 3 Stomatology</strong>
   464|                <span class="tag">CMU Partnership</strong>
   465|                <span class="tag">Digital Dentistry</strong>
   466|                
   467|                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Why patients choose it:</strong> The Ninth People's Hospital has one of the highest implant volumes in Asia. Their digital dentistry center uses 3D-printed surgical guides for precision placement. They were among the first Chinese hospitals to offer computer-guided All-on-4 procedures.</p>
   468|                
   469|                <p><strong>Implants available:</strong> Full spectrum including Straumann, Zimmer, Osstem, Dentium</p>
   470|                <p><strong>Cost range:</strong> $900 - $2,000 per implant</p>
   471|                <p><strong>Location advantage:</strong> Shanghai's international airport has direct flights from most global hubs; the hospital is 30 minutes from Pudong Airport</p>
   472|            </div>
   473|
   474|            <div class="hospital-card">
   475|                <h3>3. West China Hospital of Stomatology (Sichuan University) — Chengdu</h3>
   476|                <span class="tag">Top 5 Stomatology</strong>
   477|                <span class="tag">Research Leader</strong>
   478|                <span class="tag">Best Value Top-Tier</strong>
   479|                
   480|                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Why patients choose it:</strong> West China Stomatology has been consistently ranked among China's top dental schools for 30+ years. They publish more implant research than almost any other Chinese institution. Cost is slightly lower than Beijing or Shanghai, making it attractive for multi-implant cases.</p>
   481|                
   482|                <p><strong>Implants available:</strong> All major international brands plus own R&D implants</p>
   483|                <p><strong>Cost range:</strong> $800 - $1,800 per implant (most affordable top-tier option)</p>
